Problem

Conductors issue tickets in a crowded, moving bus, often with poor network and a printer that can jam. A slow or confusing app causes queues, and a failed print can lead to a duplicate ticket and a double charge. This design aims to:

Keep a normal sale to a few taps
Keep working when the network is down
Recover from printer failures without creating a second sale
Screen layout

Every screen shows:

Element	Purpose
Route header	Route name and current stop (e.g. "500D to Silk Board")
Status bar	Printer state (Ready / Printing / Jam) and network state (Online / Offline)
Trip total	Running collection for the trip
Main action button	Large yellow button at the bottom (e.g. "Issue ticket · ₹45")
Flows
1. Normal ticket
Pick a destination from the stop grid (Majestic, Town Hall, Jayanagar, HSR Layout and more). Each shows its per-person fare.
Choose the passenger count (1 to 5). The total updates instantly (e.g. 3 × ₹15 = ₹45).
Tap Issue ticket.
The Printing in progress screen shows the ticket details.
The Printed screen confirms destination, amount, ticket number and trip total.
Tap Next ticket to reset the screen.
2. More stops
The More stops button opens the full list of stops.
Recent stops appear first, with their fares.
Search narrows the list. If nothing matches, a No stops found state offers Clear search.
Picking a stop returns to the main screen with that stop selected.
Closing without picking keeps the existing selection.
3. Offline mode
Tickets can still be issued with no network.
The ticket is saved locally and printed as normal.
The confirmation shows Saved locally · Sync pending.
The trip total keeps updating while offline.
Saved tickets sync when the network returns.
4. Printer jam
The conductor issues a ticket (e.g. #104, 3 passengers to Majestic, ₹45).
The printer jams during printing.
A Printer jam screen shows recovery options.
The conductor clears the jam.
The app shows Jam cleared with the saved ticket details.
Reprinting in progress reprints the same ticket (#104).
Sale retained confirms the sale was counted once.
Next ticket resets the screen, and View saved ticket stays available.

Rule: a jam never creates a new sale or an extra charge. The original saved ticket is reprinted.

5. Print status unknown
If the app cannot tell whether the ticket printed (e.g. the printer lost connection), it shows Print status unknown.
It then runs Checking Ticket #104.
Reprinting is allowed only after the status is confirmed.
This prevents duplicate tickets and double charging.
Ticket states
State	What the conductor sees
Ready	Destination and passenger count selectable
Printing	Spinner with ticket details
Printed	Green confirmation with ticket number and trip total
Saved locally	Printed, with "Sync pending" (offline)
Jammed	Printer jam alert with recovery options
Reprinting	Same ticket sent to the printer again
Sale retained	Confirms the sale was counted once
Status unknown	Ticket check runs before any reprint
Design principles
Speed: a normal ticket takes a few taps.
No double charging: every recovery path reuses the original saved ticket.
Always visible status: printer and network state appear on every screen.
Works offline: nothing blocks ticket issuing.
Clear feedback: distinct states for printing, success, jam, recovery and unknown.
Glanceable: dark theme, high contrast, large numbers and buttons.
Design decisions
Dark theme with yellow accent: easy to read in changing light on a bus, and the main action stands out.
Large stop grid and number buttons: easy to tap while standing and holding on.
Selection kept on close: closing the stop search never loses what the conductor already chose.
Reuse, never recreate: a failed print is treated as a printing problem, not a new sale.
Check before reprint: when the outcome is unclear, the app verifies first.
